Finding the downstairs, digging a route from there to the upstairs, and
proceeding.  Takes like 20-30 minutes a level, then add in time spent
dropping off stashes when I get burdened and clearing
valley/towers/sanctum, and crossing it again with the bell/book/candle
on the ascension run, and climbing back out.  My last samurai liberally
used controlled levelport and ignored all but the most important loot,
she got it down to like 8 hours or so.  My monk was pretty quick with
the eyes, too.  So I'm probably getting faster, but over my 15-20
ascensions that seems like a reasonable average.

I don't really understand how real-time speed ascenders do it so fast.
I mean, if I had every level magic-mapped you figure it's still going
to be 100 turns or so on each level by the time you dig out a pathway,
cross it back and forth and have to spend some time in combat or
whatever, it's still got to be 100 turns spent on each of ~25 levels.
That's almost 2 hours if you rarely stop to think, not counting time
spent in the valley/towers/sanctum.  But they somehow do the whole game
in under 5 hours.  I can see cutting down the turns for a turn-speed
ascension by jumping, but that's going to take longer in real-time.

If giving the amulet to a pet and levelporting still works you could
save time and turns there I guess, but that's pretty much exploiting a
bug IMO so I don't do it (I don't have a problem with people who do,
it's within the rules, and I'd do it if it works for a speed ascension
since that's all about fewest turns with every trick you can think of,
but for normal play it just seems like there's a lot of code meant to
ensure you actually trek out and the pet trick is an oversight and not
an intentional loophole).



